var TOPICS = [{"n":1,"title":"Fire Dynamics & Fire Behavior","sub":"How fire actually behaves inside a structure — the vocabulary for everything else.","concepts":["Modern fires become <b>ventilation-limited</b> early: oxygen — not fuel — is the controlling factor.","<b>Vent-before-water feeds the fire;</b> water first cools and buys time. Air first buys intensity.","Your opening can <i>be</i> the ventilation event — coordinate water with entry and control doors.","Hose streams entrain air and move pressure: <b>water on the fire, not air into the room.</b>"],"videos":[{"title":"Fundamentals of Fire Science","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/124/fundamentals-of-fire-science","dur":"16 min","desc":"Fire science basics — the vocabulary everything else builds on."},{"title":"Fire Dynamics in Structures","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/125/fire-dynamics-in-structures","dur":"17 min","desc":"How fire actually behaves inside structures; foundation for this whole topic."}],"cards":[{"q":"You're first-due at 0200 on a single-family residential. All windows and doors intact, light smoke pushing from the eaves, no visible flame. Your captain orders a window taken on Side A for ventilation before the line is charged. Based on FSRI research, what happens to interior conditions in the next 60 seconds?","a":"They deteriorate fast — the fire is ventilation-limited (oxygen-starved), and the new opening feeds it: heat release rate spikes, temperatures climb rapidly, and the fire can race toward flashover.","why":"In modern fuel loads the fire becomes ventilation-limited early; oxygen is the controlling factor. Vent-before-water doesn't relieve the fire, it fuels it. Water first cools and buys time; air first buys intensity."},{"q":"Your crew advances down a hallway toward the fire room. The entry door is open behind you. What's the danger and what do you do?","a":"The open door behind you makes the hallway the flow path — you are standing between the intake and the fire, directly in the exhaust stream of superheated gases. Control that door (close it or post someone on door control) before advancing.","why":"Fire travels from high pressure to low pressure along the flow path. An uncontrolled door turns your advance route into the chimney."},{"q":"You're flowing water from the exterior through a window into the involved room (transitional attack) while the interior crew stages at the door. What does the research say happens inside, and what must the interior crew know?","a":"Interior temperatures drop hundreds of degrees in seconds and the heat release rate collapses — conditions improve dramatically. But it also generates a steam push, so the interior crew must be masked, ready to move in immediately, and aware the thermal picture just changed.","why":"Water absorbs enormous heat energy. Applied before entry it resets the thermal environment — but timing with entry is everything, and steam is the price."},{"q":"You're flowing a straight stream through a doorway into a ventilation-limited room. Besides putting water on the fire, what is your stream doing?","a":"It's entraining air and driving it ahead of the stream into the room — feeding oxygen to an oxygen-starved fire and altering the pressure/flow path.","why":"Hose streams move large volumes of air (air entrainment). In a ventilation-limited space, sloppy stream placement can intensify burning. Technique matters: put water on the fire, not air into the room."},{"q":"Thick black smoke, high heat at the door, no visible flame, windows intact. What stage is this fire in and what's your biggest concern?","a":"Ventilation-limited (under-ventilated), likely near flashover conditions. Biggest concern: your entry IS the ventilation event — opening that door can trigger rapid fire growth.","why":"Modern fires carry huge energy but little oxygen. Everything is loaded and waiting for air. Coordinate water with entry and control doors, or you become the trigger."}],"diagrams":[{"title":"The neutral plane and flow arrows","cap":"Blue air flows <b>low</b> from intake to fire; red gases flow <b>high</b> from fire to exhaust.","svg":"t1d1"},{"title":"Ventilation-limited vs. seconds after ventilation","cap":"The same room: oxygen-starved fire, then violent growth the moment air arrives.","svg":"t1d2"},{"title":"Air entrainment","cap":"The stream drags air with it. Technique matters: water on the fire, not air into the room.","svg":"t1d3"}]},{"n":2,"title":"Flow Paths & Ventilation Tactics","sub":"Intake, exhaust, and the deadly ground between them — plus when (not) to vent.","concepts":["Flow runs from high pressure to low pressure: <b>intake → fire → exhaust</b>.","Every new opening redraws the map. Between intake and fire, you stand in the exhaust stream.","<b>Water on the fire first</b> (or truly simultaneous and coordinated), then vent. Ventilation is a tactic, not a reflex.","<b>Door control is the primary defense</b> — against fire growth and wind-driven blowtorch paths."],"videos":[{"title":"Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/162/understanding-flow-paths-low-intake-high-exhaust","dur":"5 min","desc":"Intake/exhaust and what's between them; the single clearest flow-path explainer."},{"title":"Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/148/impact-of-horizontal-ventilation-on-fire-behavior","dur":"47 min","desc":"What ventilation actually does to fire behavior; the research behind water-first vs vent-first."}],"pfdNote":{"title":"PFD ventilation doctrine","src":"From the department's recruit Ventilation deck (Ch. 13)","items":["<b>Transitional attack.</b> Hit it from the outside <b>first</b>: an exterior offensive indirect handline into the compartment initiates cooling <i>before</i> transitioning to interior direct attack coordinated with ventilation — done prior to entry, search, or suppression. Prevents backdraft and flashover.","<b>The Three Ws before ventilating: When, Where, Why.</b> Timing rule: keep openings closed until you're ready to apply water — ventilation without water = fire growth. Water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates.","<b>Never position between the fire and where it wants to go</b> without a hoseline or the ability to close a door — especially basement fires.","<b>Vertical ventilation alone</b> usually has no positive effect on a ventilation-limited fire; paired with coordinated exterior water as close to the fire as possible, it improves visibility and reduces temperature.","<b>Wind:</b> keep it at your back during attack; opening the upwind side feeds unlimited high-pressure O<sub>2</sub> into the fire.","<b>Flashover signs:</b> high heat, ghosting fingers of flame, sudden lowering of the smoke layer — do <b class=\"r\">NOT</b> enter; cool it transitional first, then vent, then enter.","<b>Hydraulic ventilation</b> (narrow fog/broken stream to push smoke/heat out): most useful for clearing a room <i>after</i> the fire is under control."]},"cards":[{"q":"Single-story home, fire in a rear bedroom. Front door is your entry; the rear sliding glass door is broken out. Where's the intake, where's the exhaust, and where do you NOT want to be?","a":"Intake at the front door (low), exhaust at the rear opening (high). The flow path runs front-to-rear straight through the house — do not position, search, or stage in that hallway without water on the fire and the door controlled.","why":"Flow runs intake → fire → exhaust. Everything between the intake and the fire sits in the exhaust stream of superheated gases heading for the exit."},{"q":"Truck company is heading to the roof for vertical ventilation on a single-family with fire in the attic. The engine hasn't put water on the fire yet. Per FSRI research, what's the risk and the correct sequence?","a":"Cutting the roof before water creates a powerful new exhaust that intensifies the fire below and can pull fire toward interior crews and openings. Correct sequence: water on the fire first (or truly simultaneous and coordinated), then vent.","why":"The vertical ventilation studies show vent-before-water increases heat release and worsens conditions for interior crews. Ventilation is a tactic, not a reflex — it must be coordinated with suppression."},{"q":"Basement fire, single-family, interior stairwell is the only access. What does the below-grade fire do to your flow path and your escape route?","a":"The stairwell becomes both intake and exhaust — superheated gases and smoke rise up the stairs into the first floor. Your entry path IS the exhaust path, and your escape route is compromised the moment you descend.","why":"Below-grade fires are stack-effect driven: the only opening becomes the chimney. Crews descending walk directly into the fire's exhaust. Consider exterior access/attack and control the basement door."},{"q":"You're second-due. First-due is making entry through the front door with a line. You see a neighbor has broken out a rear window \"to help.\" What do you do?","a":"Treat it as a tactical emergency: the flow path just changed. Immediately notify command and the interior crew, and either get that opening covered or adjust the plan — do not assume the original attack plan still holds.","why":"Every new opening redefines intake and exhaust. Uncoordinated ventilation can pull fire and lethal heat toward the new exhaust — possibly onto the crew inside. Ventilation must be coordinated, never random."},{"q":"25 mph wind at your back as you advance through the front door, fire in the rear of the house. What's the specific danger and the counter-tactic?","a":"Wind creates a forced blowtorch flow path from your entry (windward) to leeward openings — fire and 1000°+ gases driven down the hallway faster than crews can react. Counter: control the entry door, compartmentalize by closing interior doors, and never advance into a wind-driven exhaust path without water.","why":"Wind overrides natural buoyancy entirely. Door control is the primary defense — a closed door between you and a wind-driven fire is often the only thing that works."},{"q":"PFD doctrine: before taking a window or cutting a roof, what are the Three Ws and what's the timing rule?","a":"When, Where, Why — and keep openings closed until you're ready to apply water. Ventilation without water = fire growth.","why":"Water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates. Once water has slowed the fire, ventilation works for you instead of against you."},{"q":"You're assigned vertical ventilation on a ventilation-limited house fire, but the engine hasn't put water on the fire yet. What's the likely effect of cutting the roof right now — and what changes that?","a":"Little to no positive effect — the new exhaust just feeds the oxygen-starved fire. Pair the cut with coordinated exterior water as close to the fire as possible, and it improves visibility and drops temperatures.","why":"PFD/UL-NIST research: venting alone on a ventilation-limited fire grows it. Water first resets the thermal environment; then ventilation helps instead of hurts."}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Floor-plan flow path","cap":"Blue intake arrows, red exhaust arrows — and the hallway between them, shaded as danger ground.","svg":"t2d1"},{"title":"Basement fire stairwell stack","cap":"Below grade, the stairwell becomes the chimney — your entry path is the fire's exhaust path.","svg":"t2d2"},{"title":"Wind-driven flow path","cap":"Wind turns your doorway into a blowtorch. Never advance into the exhaust.","svg":"t2d3"}]},{"n":3,"title":"Reading Smoke","sub":"Volume, velocity, density, color — the four things smoke is telling the first-due officer.","concepts":["Read <b>four</b> attributes: volume (extent), velocity (heat/pressure), density (fuel richness), color (fuel type and toxicity).","<b>Brown banking smoke</b> and pulsing/\"breathing\" doorways are pre-flashover warnings.","Yellowish, acrid smoke = synthetics and toxins (HCN). Full SCBA, zero skin exposure — search urgency goes <b>up</b>, not down.","Cleaner smoke after ventilation can mean a <b>bigger</b> fire, not a dying one. Read continuously, never once."],"videos":[{"title":"Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/98/impact-of-ventilation-on-fire-patterns","dur":"90 min","desc":"How ventilation changes what you see; the closest FSRI analog to dedicated reading-smoke training."},{"title":"Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/105/residential-fire-near-miss-incident-in-lagrange-ga","dur":"65 min","desc":"A real near-miss where crews misread conditions; practice spotting what they missed before the reveal."}],"cards":[{"q":"From the front lawn: high-volume, high-velocity, turbulent BLACK smoke pushing hard from the eaves. Break down what volume, velocity, density, and color each tell you.","a":"Volume = extent of burning (a lot of fuel involved). Velocity = pressure/heat driving it (fast = intense heat). Density (thick) = fuel-rich, incomplete combustion. Black = hydrocarbon/petroleum-based fuels. Combined: a large, hot, ventilation-limited fire — expect violent change the moment it's ventilated.","why":"The four attributes each read a different variable — volume is extent, velocity is pressure and heat, density is fuel richness, color is fuel type and combustion efficiency. Read all four, not just one."},{"q":"Thin, wispy white-gray smoke drifting lazily from a soffit vent — versus thick BROWN smoke banking down inside the hallway. Contrast the two.","a":"The exterior smoke says early-stage or smoldering, lower heat. The brown banking smoke inside is the killer: brown means wood pyrolysis products at high temperature, and banking down to floor level means heat is filling the space from the ceiling down — a pre-flashover warning.","why":"Color tracks fuel and temperature; velocity tracks pressure; height tracks heat. Brown + low = the room is about to go."},{"q":"Smoke is pulsing — breathing in and out of the front doorway, inhaling then exhaling. What is this and what do you do?","a":"Textbook precursor to flashover or backdraft: the fire is gasping at the edge of its available oxygen. Do NOT enter through that door without water ready — cool the space first (transitional attack through a window if possible) and control the door.","why":"Pulsing means the fire is oxygen-limited and unstable. Your entry provides the air for explosive fire growth. This is the warning shot — heed it."},{"q":"Yellowish-gray smoke with a sharp acrid smell at a commercial occupancy. What does the color suggest and how does it change your urgency?","a":"Yellowish tint suggests nitrogenous/toxic products — synthetics, chemicals, or mixed commercial fuels producing hydrogen cyanide and other toxins. Full SCBA discipline, zero skin exposure, and search urgency goes UP: survivability in that atmosphere drops to minutes.","why":"Color hints at fuel chemistry, and chemistry determines toxicity. Reading smoke isn't just about fire behavior — it's a victim-survivability calculation."},{"q":"You vent a window and the outside smoke quickly goes from black and turbulent to lighter and faster-moving. Good sign?","a":"Not necessarily — it often means you've relieved pressure and the fire is now getting air and burning MORE completely and intensely. Cleaner-looking smoke after ventilation can mean a bigger fire, not a dying one. Verify with thermal conditions and keep applying water.","why":"Ventilation changes combustion efficiency. Smoke reading is continuous, not a snapshot — never declare victory off one change in appearance."}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Smoke-reading reference chart","cap":"Four classics: early smoldering, large ventilation-limited, pre-flashover banking, pulse warning.","svg":"t3d1"},{"title":"Banked smoke and the thermal layer","cap":"Smoke banking down = heat filling from ceiling down. Stay below the layer.","svg":"t3d2"}]},{"n":4,"title":"Building Construction & Collapse","sub":"Construction type is your timeline — plus voids, cocklofts, and the roof that kills.","concepts":["<b>Construction type is your timeline.</b> Lightweight engineered trusses and I-joists can fail in 5–10 minutes under fire.","Type V = wood throughout; Type III = masonry exterior, wood interior. Read it off the walls.","<b>Cocklofts and concealed voids</b> spread fire unseen — pull ceilings and check voids early.","<b>Collapse indicators are lagging:</b> spongy floors, sagging roof, moving walls = withdraw immediately, collapse zone at 1.5× height."],"videos":[{"title":"Building Construction","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/133/building-construction","dur":"12 min","desc":"Construction types; the taxonomy for everything in this topic."},{"title":"Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/76/structural-stability-of-engineered-lumber-in-fire-conditions","dur":"31 min","desc":"Engineered vs dimensional collapse timelines with the actual research data."}],"cards":[{"q":"Size-up: 2019-built single-family, fire in the attic, truss roof, lightweight engineered I-joists. What does the construction tell you about your timeline and tactics?","a":"Engineered lightweight components fail catastrophically fast — unprotected lightweight trusses and I-joists can collapse in as little as 5–10 minutes of fire exposure. Tactics: no prolonged interior ops above or below involved lightweight truss; go defensive earlier; establish a collapse zone.","why":"Engineered lumber has less mass and adhesives that fail at lower temperatures — FSRI research shows dramatically shorter collapse times than dimensional lumber. The building's age and construction type IS your timeline."},{"q":"Type V wood-frame vs Type III ordinary: you're looking at a 3-story apartment building. How do you tell them apart on size-up and why does it matter?","a":"Type V = wood frame throughout, everything combustible. Type III = masonry/noncombustible exterior walls with wood interior (joists, roof) — read it from the exterior: brick or block walls suggest III. It matters because fire spread and collapse differ: in III the walls stand while interior floors and roof burn and drop; void spaces, parapets, and concealed spaces drive the tactics.","why":"Construction type predicts both fire behavior and collapse mode. It drives the entire strategy — get it wrong and you're fighting the wrong building."},{"q":"Cockloft — define it, say where you find it, and explain why it kills firefighters.","a":"The concealed space between the top-floor ceiling and the roof deck, common in taxpayers, strip commercial, and older multi-family. It kills because fire runs undetected horizontally through it across the whole building, then drops down or causes sudden roof collapse on crews working below who never saw it coming.","why":"Concealed spaces defeat direct attack and hide fire spread. By the time a cockloft fire is visible, structural members are already compromised — hence early void inspection and pulling ceilings."},{"q":"Interior crew reports spongy floors. From outside you see the roofline sagging and a fresh crack in a load-bearing wall. Your call?","a":"Withdraw everyone immediately, switch to defensive, establish a collapse zone at 1.5x building height. Spongy floors = floor system failing; sagging roofline = truss/roof compromise; wall cracks = structural movement.","why":"Collapse indicators are lagging — by the time they're visible, failure is imminent, not approaching. The only safe action is evacuation. No property is worth a firefighter."},{"q":"Bowstring truss roof, commercial building, heavy fire showing. What's special about this roof and your tactics?","a":"Bowstring (arched) trusses are infamous for sudden, total, outward collapse with little warning — when one member fails the whole system unzips and pushes walls out. Tactics: no crews under a burning bowstring truss roof, defensive from the start if the truss is involved, big collapse zone.","why":"A historic firefighter killer with multiple LODD incidents behind it. Unlike other roofs it gives almost no sagging warning — the arch geometry fails all at once."}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Dimensional lumber vs. engineered components","cap":"Massive wood chars slowly; thin members and adhesives fail fast — collapse in minutes.","svg":"t4d1"},{"title":"The cockloft","cap":"Hidden horizontal highway for fire between ceiling and roof deck. Check voids early.","svg":"t4d2"},{"title":"Bowstring truss collapse","cap":"The arch unzips all at once and throws the walls outward. Big collapse zone, defensive early.","svg":"t4d3"}]},{"n":5,"title":"SCBA & Air Management","sub":"Buddy checks, the rule of thirds, low-air discipline, and the mayday math.","concepts":["<b>Low-air alarm = begin exit immediately.</b> Reserve is survival margin, not working time.","Rule of thirds: a third in, a third out, a third reserve. Turn before you burn the margin.","A downed firefighter gets your <b>Mayday (LUNAR)</b> — but never become the second victim.","<b>Looks clear ≠ is clear:</b> mask up during overhaul until the atmosphere is metered clean."],"videos":[{"title":"FSRI YouTube channel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/firesafetyresearchinstitute","dur":"channel","desc":"Search the channel for SCBA and air-management experiment footage. No SCBA-specific course exists in the FSRI plan, so the channel is the honest source."}],"inHouseRef":"In-house reference: the department's own SCBA doctrine lives in the PFD Training library — <b>Recruit Training &gt; PowerPoints &gt; \"SCBA PP Presentation joe bonnell.pptx\"</b>.","pfdNote":{"src":"From the department's recruit SCBA deck — Scott Air-Pak X3 Pro","items":["<b>Cylinders.</b> 4500 psi, 45-minute, snap-change threaded fitting. Pressure lights: <b class=\"g\">2 green</b> = 76&ndash;100%, <b class=\"g\">1 green</b> = 51&ndash;75%, <b class=\"y\">1 yellow</b> = 36&ndash;50% (prepare to exit), <b class=\"r\">1 red</b> = 35% or less (leave immediately). Vibralert low-air alarm — audible <i>and</i> tactile — sounds at ~35%.","<b>PASS.</b> Pre-alarm after 20 seconds motionless, full alarm 12 seconds after that (or hit the red manual button).","<b>SOP (Vol 2).</b> SCBA SHALL be used in contaminated, may-become-contaminated, O2-deficient, or suspected atmospheres — explicitly including the active fire area, directly above it, gas leaks/fuel spills, visible products of combustion (even vehicle/dumpster fires), and untested confined spaces.","<b>Malfunction.</b> Red-tag it, report to the Captain, replace immediately; batteries changed only by a certified SCBA tech. Every member checks their SCBA at start of each shift and after each use."]},"cards":[{"q":"Your low-air alarm activates while you're searching a second-floor bedroom. Immediate action — and why is \"a few more minutes\" wrong?","a":"Begin exit IMMEDIATELY toward your entry point or hoseline; notify your partner and command. \"A few more minutes\" is wrong because on the Scott X3 Pro your Vibralert sounds at ~35% — that's roughly one-third of your cylinder left, and under exertion that's minutes of working air, not a comfort margin. Exiting through deteriorating conditions always takes longer than you think.","why":"The alarm means you're on reserve, and reserve is survival margin, not working time. Most SCBA emergencies come from delayed exits, not empty cylinders."},{"q":"What's the rule of thumb for managing your air on entry, and why that ratio?","a":"The rule of thirds (or the department's air management policy): roughly a third of your air to work your way in, a third to get out, a third in reserve — check pressure at entry and turn when you've used your working portion.","why":"You must be able to exit under worst-case conditions — disoriented, low visibility, dragging a victim. The reserve isn't extra time; it's the margin that gets you home when everything goes wrong."},{"q":"Your partner's PASS is sounding and he's not answering the radio. Your own low-air alarm just activated. What takes priority and what's the procedure?","a":"The downed firefighter takes priority — call an immediate Mayday with LUNAR and his last known location so RIT deploys with full cylinders. But you cannot become the second victim: if you can't effect the rescue on your remaining air, withdraw and guide the rescue team in rather than dying next to him.","why":"A downed firefighter with no air is a rescue; a rescuer with no air is a recovery. Air discipline applies even during rescues — call it early."},{"q":"Overhaul. The smoke has mostly cleared and the crew wants masks off. Your call?","a":"Masks stay on until the atmosphere is metered and verified clear. \"Looks clear\" means nothing — overhaul atmospheres carry lethal CO and particulates with no visible smoke.","why":"Carbon monoxide and hydrogen cyanide persist at toxic levels long after visible smoke clears. Smoke exposure during overhaul is a leading contributor to firefighter cardiac events and long-term illness. Meter it or mask up."},{"q":"Buddy check before entry — what are you actually verifying on your partner's SCBA, and why does each item matter?","a":"Cylinder pressure (full — determines working time), PASS armed (no distress signal if he's downed), facepiece seal and regulator function (bad seal = smoke inhalation), HUD working (air awareness), radio on the right channel (can't call Mayday otherwise).","why":"Each is a failure point that kills. The two-minute check before entry prevents the fatal five minutes inside."}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Buddy check","cap":"Verify before every entry: full cylinder, armed PASS, facepiece seal, working HUD, correct channel.","svg":"t5d1"},{"title":"Rule of thirds","cap":"A third in, a third out, a third reserve. The alarm means EXIT — not more working time.","svg":"t5d2"},{"title":"Overhaul atmosphere","cap":"Looks clear is not is clear. Overhaul produces toxic CO — mask up or meter it.","svg":"t5d3"}]},{"n":6,"title":"Medical Spanish — First Contact","sub":"High-value questions, instructions, and words you may hear during the first minutes of patient care.","concepts":["Start simple: <b>Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarle.</b> — I’m a firefighter. I’m here to help you.","Ask one short question at a time. <b>¿Qué pasó?</b> (What happened?) and <b>¿Dónde le duele?</b> (Where does it hurt?) open the assessment.","Listen for urgent phrases: <b>no puedo respirar</b> (I can’t breathe), <b>dolor en el pecho</b> (chest pain), <b>me desmayé</b> (I fainted), and <b>estoy sangrando</b> (I’m bleeding).","Use these phrases to begin care, then bring in a qualified interpreter as soon as practical. This deck does not replace your medical protocols or a complete assessment."],"videos":[],"pfdNote":{"title":"Quick phrase card","src":"Plain-language Spanish for initial patient contact","items":["<b>¿Qué pasó?</b> — What happened? &nbsp; <b>¿Dónde le duele?</b> — Where does it hurt?","<b>¿Tiene dolor en el pecho?</b> — Do you have chest pain? &nbsp; <b>¿Le falta el aire?</b> — Are you short of breath?","<b>¿Cuándo empezó?</b> — When did it start? &nbsp; <b>¿Fue de repente?</b> — Was it sudden?","<b>¿Toma medicamentos?</b> — Do you take medications? &nbsp; <b>¿Tiene alergias?</b> — Do you have allergies?","<b>No se mueva.</b> — Don’t move. &nbsp; <b>Respire despacio.</b> — Breathe slowly. &nbsp; <b>Apriete mi mano.</b> — Squeeze my hand."]},"cards":[{"q":"You make first contact with a Spanish-speaking patient. Say: ‘I’m a firefighter. I’m here to help you. What happened?’","a":"Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarle. ¿Qué pasó?","why":"A short introduction establishes your role and intent, then opens the assessment without stacking several questions at once."},{"q":"A patient says, ‘Me duele el pecho y no puedo respirar.’ What are they telling you?","a":"‘My chest hurts and I can’t breathe.’ The key words are dolor/duele (pain/hurts), pecho (chest), and no puedo respirar (I can’t breathe).","why":"Those two complaints are high-priority cues. Recognizing the phrase should immediately focus your assessment while you follow protocol and arrange interpretation."},{"q":"Ask the patient where it hurts. Then name four answers you should recognize: head, chest, abdomen, and back.","a":"¿Dónde le duele? Listen for: la cabeza (head), el pecho (chest), el abdomen or el estómago (abdomen/stomach), and la espalda (back).","why":"The pattern ‘Me duele…’ means ‘My … hurts.’ Body-location words let you understand the first answer even before a full interpreter-assisted history."},{"q":"Ask: ‘When did it start? Was it sudden or little by little?’","a":"¿Cuándo empezó? ¿Fue de repente o poco a poco?","why":"Cuándo asks when; empezó means started; de repente means suddenly; poco a poco means gradually."},{"q":"You need medication and allergy history. Ask both questions in Spanish.","a":"¿Toma medicamentos? ¿Tiene alergias? If needed: ¿A qué? means ‘To what?’","why":"Medicamentos and alergias are close to their English equivalents, making them useful anchor words in a time-sensitive history."},{"q":"A trauma patient says, ‘Me caí, me golpeé la cabeza y me desmayé.’ Translate it.","a":"‘I fell, I hit my head, and I fainted/lost consciousness.’ Me caí = I fell; me golpeé la cabeza = I hit my head; me desmayé = I fainted.","why":"These are common phrases you may hear after a fall and each changes what you need to clarify during the trauma assessment."},{"q":"Give three simple neurologic-assessment instructions: ‘Smile. Raise both arms. Squeeze my hands.’","a":"Sonría. Levante los dos brazos. Apriete mis manos.","why":"Short, one-step commands are easier to understand and let you observe facial symmetry, arm drift, and grip while language help is arranged."},{"q":"Ask a patient: ‘Do you know where you are? Do you know what day it is?’","a":"¿Sabe dónde está? ¿Sabe qué día es hoy?","why":"These questions help test orientation. Sabe means ‘do you know,’ dónde means ‘where,’ and hoy means ‘today.’"},{"q":"Ask: ‘Are you pregnant? How many weeks? Is there bleeding?’","a":"¿Está embarazada? ¿Cuántas semanas? ¿Hay sangrado?","why":"Embarazada means pregnant; semanas means weeks; sangrado means bleeding. Ask clearly and privately when possible."},{"q":"You need the patient still and calm. Say: ‘Don’t move. Breathe slowly. I’m going to help you.’","a":"No se mueva. Respire despacio. Voy a ayudarle.","why":"These are respectful formal commands. Mueva means move, respire means breathe, and despacio means slowly."}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Body-location word map","cap":"Point and listen: cabeza, cuello, pecho, abdomen, espalda, brazo, and pierna.","svg":"t6d1"}]},{"n":7,"title":"Academy Skills","sub":"The core fireground skills drilled in the academy — SCBA, ventilation, search & rescue, fire behavior, ladders, and live fire — taught the way a PFD recruit learns them.","concepts":["<b>SCBA usage.</b> The Scott Air-Pak X3 Pro is your lifeline: 4500 psi, 45-minute cylinders, Vibralert at ~35%. Air discipline is non-negotiable.","<b>Ventilation.</b> The Three Ws — When, Where, Why — and the timing rule: no openings until water is ready.","<b>Search &amp; rescue.</b> Oriented, systematic search; VES with the door closed; the TIC extends your senses, never replaces them.","<b>Advanced fire behavior.</b> Modern fires go ventilation-limited early and punish air-first tactics. Read backdraft and flashover signs before they read you.","<b>Ladder operations.</b> 4:1 angle, heeled and locked, placed for the rescue window — never over the fire.","<b>Live fire scenarios.</b> The burn building is where the other five skills meet: coordinated attack, honest reads, and the discipline to back out."],"pfdNote":{"title":"Where this comes from","src":"City of Phoenix Legistar File #24-2678 — “Phoenix Fire Department Academy Overview”","items":["The city’s academy overview lists these same skills among the recruit fundamentals: SCBA usage, ventilation, search and rescue, fire behavior, ladder operations, and live fire.","These drills aren’t built from the city’s summary document — they’re built on the department doctrine already in this app: PFD SOP Vol 2 (SCBA) and the recruit Ventilation deck, Ch. 13.","Drill them until they’re automatic. The academy teaches the skill; the fireground tests it."]},"videos":[],"cards":[{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Academy burn-building evolution. Your HUD shows one yellow pressure light — 36–50% — and your partner says there’s plenty of air left to finish the search. Your call?","a":"He’s wrong — you start planning your exit now. On the X3 Pro, 1 yellow means 36–50% remaining, and under real exertion that disappears fast. The next step down is the red light at 35% with the Vibralert screaming, and that means LEAVE — not ‘a few more minutes.’","why":"The HUD is a countdown, not a gauge: 2 green is 76–100%, 1 green 51–75%, 1 yellow 36–50% (prepare to exit), 1 red 35% or less (leave immediately). Recruits consistently overestimate what half a cylinder buys under work rate — respect the yellow."},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Interior search: your low-air alarm activates — audible AND tactile Vibralert. Your partner is mid-bedroom and says ‘almost done.’ What’s the move, and why does the double signal matter?","a":"Exit begins immediately — both of you head for your entry point or hoseline and notify command. The double signal exists because one sense can fail you: in a roaring IDLH you might not hear the beep, but you will feel the shake. It fires at ~35%, which is reserve air — survival margin, not working time.","why":"Same discipline as Topic 5: the alarm means you’re on the last third of your air. ‘Almost done’ has killed firefighters — exiting through deteriorating conditions always takes longer than the search you’re finishing."},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"You go motionless about 25 seconds while your partner checks a closet. Your PASS gives its pre-alarm chirp, and he snaps that you’ll set it off if you keep stopping. What do you tell him?","a":"Good — that’s exactly what it’s supposed to do. Pre-alarm sounds after 20 seconds motionless, full alarm 12 seconds after that, and there’s a manual red button for when you need it now. A PASS that nags you is a PASS that’s working; the one time it matters, you want it loud.","why":"PASS discipline: armed before entry, respected during ops. Firefighters who silence or ignore motion alarms remove the one device that calls for them when they can’t call for themselves."},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Knockdown is done on a dumpster fire. The smoke has mostly cleared and the crew wants masks off to pull the pile apart. PFD SOP Vol 2 says what?","a":"Masks stay on. SOP requires SCBA in atmospheres that are contaminated or may become contaminated — and it names visible products of combustion explicitly, including vehicle and dumpster fires, plus gas leaks, fuel spills, the active fire area and directly above it. Overhaul on a smoldering pile is a contamination event waiting to happen.","why":"Looks clear is not is clear, and SOP Vol 2 doesn’t leave it to judgment — the listed atmospheres get SCBA, period. Dumpster and vehicle fires are called out by name because crews habitually underrate them."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Academy evolution: your crew is assigned to take windows on Side A while the engine is still stretching the line. The instructor asks for the Three Ws first. What are they, and what timing rule do they enforce?","a":"When, Where, Why — and the rule is: keep openings closed until you’re ready to apply water. Ventilation without water equals fire growth. The Ws force you to justify the opening against a water plan instead of venting on reflex.","why":"Straight from the recruit Vent Ch. 13: water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates. Once water is applied, ventilation works for you; before that, it works for the fire. Most recruits vent because it feels like doing something — the Three Ws make it a tactic."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Burn building, ventilation-limited room fire. The instructor has you hit it with an exterior offensive indirect handline through the window FIRST, before anyone goes inside. What is this called, and which two killers does it prevent?","a":"Transitional attack — exterior cooling before transitioning to interior direct attack, coordinated with ventilation, done prior to entry, search, or suppression. It prevents backdraft and flashover by collapsing the heat release rate before crews commit inside.","why":"PFD recruit ventilation doctrine: interior temperatures can drop hundreds of degrees in seconds. Your entry is the ventilation event — transitional attack makes sure the fire is already losing when you open the door, instead of waiting for you to feed it."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Live burn, 20 mph wind hitting the same side as your entry door. The instructor asks: where do you want the wind, and what happens if you open the upwind side?","a":"Keep the wind at your back during attack. Opening the upwind side feeds unlimited high-pressure oxygen into the fire and turns the structure into a blowtorch aimed downwind — straight at anyone inside or exiting on the lee side.","why":"PFD doctrine. Wind doesn’t nudge the flow path, it owns it — it overrides natural buoyancy entirely. Windward openings are fuel; door control and wind-aware positioning are the only defenses."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"A classmate says: ‘The room is oxygen-starved, so let’s cut the roof — ventilation can only help.’ Using recruit vent doctrine, why is he wrong, and when does the cut actually help?","a":"He’s wrong because on a ventilation-limited fire, vertical ventilation alone usually has no positive effect — the new exhaust just feeds the oxygen-starved fire. The cut helps when paired with coordinated exterior water applied as close to the fire as possible: then it improves visibility and drops temperatures.","why":"The research the department teaches: venting alone grows a ventilation-limited fire. The roof cut is a tool for a cooled fire, not a substitute for cooling. Sequence is the doctrine."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Burn-building search, zero visibility. Your partner loses the wall and starts free-searching the middle of the room ‘to cover more ground.’ What’s wrong, and what’s the fix?","a":"He’s unoriented — free-searching the middle of a room is how firefighters die in buildings they can’t see. Fix: re-establish contact with the wall or the hoseline, re-orient to your entry point, and search systematically off a known reference. Coverage means nothing without orientation.","why":"Academy search doctrine: stay oriented to something that leads out — wall, line, rope. An unoriented searcher isn’t searching, they’re wandering, and a wanderer becomes the second victim RIT has to find."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"You’re assigned VES on a second-floor bedroom window in a live burn: vent, enter, search. Your partner wants to leave the bedroom door open ‘for a second way out.’ Correct him.","a":"Wrong — the door stays closed. VES works because the closed door isolates the room from the fire and the flow path; you vent the window, enter, and search from a tenable space. Opening the door invites the fire and its exhaust straight into your search area — and turns your window into the exhaust.","why":"VES is a disciplined, isolated tactic: window in, door closed, search, window out. The second way out is the window you came through. An open door doesn’t add an exit, it adds a flow path."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Your crew has a TIC on the search. The camera shows a clear room and your officer waves you ahead faster. What’s the right use of the camera — and what mistake is the officer making?","a":"The TIC is a scanning tool, not a substitute for contact, orientation, and disciplined technique. Use it to scan and prioritize, but stay low, keep your reference, and search to the standard. The mistake: outrunning your orientation because the picture looks clean — cameras miss victims, don’t show floor integrity, and die at the worst moment.","why":"The academy lesson: the TIC extends your senses, it doesn’t replace your fundamentals. Every generation finds a new technology to trust more than its training. Don’t be that evolution."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Second-floor search, conditions deteriorating fast, and you realize you don’t know which way the stairs are. Immediate action — and what should have prevented this?","a":"Stop, call it out, and declare a Mayday with LUNAR early — while you still have air and composure — so RIT comes with full cylinders to your last known location. What should have prevented it: continuous orientation — counting doors, wall and line contact, and a pre-entry plan with an exit reference.","why":"Lost firefighters die from delayed Maydays, not from being lost. Call it while you can still talk and breathe. And the prevention is boring on purpose: orientation is a habit, not a skill you summon once you’re already lost."},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"Academy classroom: why do modern house fires reach flashover faster than the legacy fires in the old textbooks? Give the two-part answer.","a":"One: modern fuel loads — synthetics, open floor plans, lightweight construction — release heat far faster than legacy natural-fiber furnishings. Two: tighter, energy-efficient homes make fires ventilation-limited early, so the moment air arrives — your entry, a window failing — the stored energy releases violently. Faster to flashover AND more explosive when ventilated.","why":"This is the ‘advanced’ in advanced fire behavior: not just hotter fires, but a different fire. Legacy tactics assumed fuel-limited fires with time; modern fires are oxygen-limited with a hair trigger. Everything else in the academy skill set assumes you understand this."},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"During a live burn you see smoke pulsing — breathing in and out of the doorway — and hear a high-pitched whistling from a cracked window. A classmate wants to push in. What are you seeing, and what’s the order?","a":"Backdraft indicators: the pulsing means the fire is gasping at the edge of its oxygen supply, and the whistling is air being drawn through the crack. Order: do NOT open that door — cool the space first, transitional attack through an opening if possible, and control doors. His entry would be the ignition event.","why":"A ventilation-limited, superheated compartment is a bomb waiting for air. Backdraft doesn’t negotiate and doesn’t warn twice. The recruit who recognizes the signs and stops the crew just earned their place."},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"The instructor keeps repeating: ‘Your entry IS the ventilation event.’ In a ventilation-limited structure, explain exactly what opening the front door does to the fire — and what must be true before you open it.","a":"The open door becomes a new intake: fresh oxygen floods an oxygen-starved, superheated compartment, the heat release rate spikes, and the fire races toward the new exhaust — possibly to flashover. Before opening it, water must be ready and applied — transitional or immediate direct attack — with doors behind you controlled so you aren’t standing in the new flow path.","why":"This ties the academy skills together: fire behavior explains WHY the ventilation and attack doctrines exist. Every tactic you’ve learned is a different answer to the same fact — air is the trigger, and you’re the one carrying it in."},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"Academy drill: raise a 24-foot extension ladder to a second-floor window for rescue. Walk through the placement — angle, footing, securing.","a":"Butt it on firm, level ground; raise to a 75-degree climbing angle — the 4:1 rule, butt out one foot for every four feet of working length; heel it or keep it heeled, tie off the halyard, and lock the pawls before anyone climbs.","why":"Ladder fundamentals are pass/fail for a reason: wrong angle means it slides out or tips back, unlocked dogs mean it collapses under load, and an unheeled butt on slick ground is a fall waiting for both the victim and the rescuer."},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"Fire showing from a second-floor window, victim reported at the adjacent bedroom window. Your partner starts raising the ladder to the fire window ‘to get water on it faster.’ Why is this wrong — and where does the ladder go?","a":"Wrong window. Ladders go to the rescue window first — the victim’s window — not the fire window. Placing at the fire exposes ladder and climbers to direct flame and heat, puts the rescue in the exhaust path, and delays the actual rescue. The fire window gets the line; the victim window gets the ladder.","why":"Academy priority order: rescue first, and the ladder is a rescue tool before it’s an access tool. Heat-weakened metal fails without warning — never climb a ladder that’s been in direct flame."},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"You need to work from the ladder at the roofline, pulling siding to check for extension. What’s the technique that keeps you on the ladder — and what are your free hands doing?","a":"Leg lock: climb a few rungs above your work point, pass one leg behind the rungs and hook the foot, leaning your weight into the lock — that frees both hands. With them you’re pulling siding, sounding the roof, or running a tool. Never work one-handed and unsecured at height.","why":"The leg lock is the difference between working from the ladder and falling off it. Recruits rush it; veterans do it every single time, even for ‘just a second’ — because ‘just a second’ is when gravity collects."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"First live-burn evolution: you’re on the nozzle approaching the fire room. Fog or straight stream — and where does the water go first?","a":"Straight stream for reach and penetration, applied to the seat of the fire from the unburned side — cool the overhead and the fire in a controlled pattern. Wide fog in the approach just makes steam that cooks your own crew and pushes heat; save fog patterns for hydraulic ventilation after knockdown.","why":"The burn building punishes textbook answers applied blindly. A straight stream reaches the seat through the thermal layer; fog near the nozzle in a hot hallway converts to a steam burn on your crew. Technique follows conditions, not habit."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Interior crew is at the burn-room door ready to enter. The exterior crew is still setting up their transitional line. Go or wait? What’s the coordinated answer, and why?","a":"Wait. Coordinated attack means exterior water hits first — collapsing the heat release rate — and the interior crew enters on the improved conditions, timed with ventilation. Entering early makes the interior crew the ventilation event. Thirty seconds of patience buys survivable conditions.","why":"Live fire rewards coordination and punishes heroics. The crew that waits for the water and enters together on a cooled fire looks slow and goes home. The crew that rushes looks aggressive and gets burned."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Mid-evolution: heat building fast at the ceiling, the smoke layer dropping, a low roar underneath it all. Your instinct says push a little further. What does the instructor want to hear instead?","a":"‘Backing out.’ Those are deteriorating conditions trending toward flashover — dropping neutral plane, rising heat, audible warning. Withdraw to a safe area, report conditions, and re-attack with water from a tenable position. There is no shame in backing out; there is a line-of-duty death in pushing through.","why":"The burn building is the one place you’re allowed to learn this lesson safely — the instructors are watching for exactly this decision. Firefighters who can’t back out in training won’t back out on the fireground, and that’s where it turns fatal."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Live-fire search evolution: charged line at the door, a partner, victim reported in the rear bedroom. How do you move — and what protects the victim from your own attack?","a":"Stay low below the thermal layer, oriented to the wall or line, moving fast and systematically — search the bedroom while the line protects the hallway and your exit. And coordinate with the nozzle: water goes on the fire, never blindly into the search area — steam and driven heat kill victims faster than the fire you were trying to beat.","why":"Live-fire search is the whole academy skill set in one evolution: SCBA discipline, fire-behavior reads, ventilation awareness, and the attack protecting the search. The victim’s survivability clock is running — but a panicked, uncoordinated search just adds bodies to the count."}],"diagrams":[]}];

